 CO3.4  FRAME ALLOCATION FOR THE 1,200-SESSION CloudMatrix MIX
==============================================================================
   Total frames (32 GB / 4 KB)   : 8,388,608
   Hypervisor + page cache (10%) : 838,860
   Available to guests  m        : 7,549,748  (28.80 GB)

   Tier                            Guests  WSS (MB)    frames   tier demand      GB
   Web / DNS microservice             900        16     4,096     3,686,400   14.06
   Interactive enterprise app         240        48    12,288     2,949,120   11.25
   Database guest VM                   40       192    49,152     1,966,080    7.50
   Batch analytics worker              20       160    40,960       819,200    3.12
   TOTAL DEMAND  D = sum WSS_i       1200                         9,420,800   35.94

   D / m = 9,420,800 / 7,549,748 = 1.248   -> +24.8% over-committed
   D > m, so the naive allocation thrashes. Three reclaim mechanisms
   close the gap, in the order the controller should apply them:

   1. KSM page sharing across 900 near-identical web guests (35%)   - 1,290,240 frames  (4.92 GB)
   2. Balloon reclaim of cold pages in the enterprise tier (10%)   -   294,912 frames  (1.12 GB)
   3. Suspend the batch analytics tier when PFF > upper threshold  -   819,200 frames  (3.12 GB)

   Residual demand D' = 7,016,448 frames (26.77 GB)
   D' / m = 0.929   -> 7.1% headroom  FITS

   Resulting per-guest allocation rule (proportional + WS floor):
     frames_i = max( WSS_i(Delta) , (size_i / sum size) * m )
     subject to  sum frames_i <= m, batch tier suspendable

   PFF control band (Denning; Wulf's page-fault-frequency scheme):
     PFF_i > U = 0.50 faults/ms  ->  grant guest i more frames
     PFF_i < L = 0.10 faults/ms  ->  balloon frames back from guest i
     PFF_i > U and no free frame ->  SUSPEND lowest-priority guest
   Batch analytics is the designated victim class: it carries no
   interactive SLA, so suspending it protects the sub-10 ms response
   time that Section D requires of the web/DNS and enterprise tiers.

